 | OQ-MET-12573 | **WHEN A PARTY ALREADY HAS A MASTER ACCOUNT AT ONE ADDRESS AND A CLERK ADDS A SECOND ADDRESS FOR THEM, SHOULD THE FORM FIND THAT ACCOUNT — OR ASK FOR THE IDENTIFIER AGAIN?** ⚠ **NOTHING IS BLOCKED AND NOTHING IS STUBBED. THE SHIPPED BEHAVIOUR IS COMPLETE AND IS WRITTEN DOWN** — the form asks again, an account opens, and `customers` resolves the party by the IDENTIFIER, so the two accounts belong to one customer and no duplicate person is created. **WHAT CHANGED AND WHY IT IS ASKED NOW:** until `BR-MET-1181` (PO ruling 2026-09-22) the declared link lived on the OWNER RECORD, so a party's second address reused the first one's account and the identity ask was **once per OWNER**. The ruling moves the declaration onto the PREMISES and takes owner records out of every screen — *"owner IDs are not required hence no screens or fucntionality for it"* — so for a party with no owner record the ask is now **once per ADDRESS**. That is a real cost to a clerk entering a landlord's twelve buildings and it is stated here rather than discovered at a counter. **WHY IT IS NOT DECIDED BY THIS LANE:** the obvious fix is to match the party by the NAME somebody typed, and **`BR-MET-151` clause 3 forbids exactly that** — a name in a box is a NAME and not a party. The honest options each cost something only the PO can weigh: **(a)** leave it — the identifier is typed again, `customers` matches the party, and one customer holds twelve accounts; **(b)** the form offers accounts the identifier ALREADY matches, once it has been typed, so the clerk attaches rather than opens (this is `master_account_id` on the payload, which is already built — what is missing is a picker, and `CR-130-BRC07` part three's Premises detail screen is where one would go); **(c)** reinstate a party-level link, which is the owner record the PO has just ruled out of the screens and would need that ruling reversed. | product · money path (who a second building is billed through) | lane C1 of `CR-130-BRC0