h is hard-won law 13's third case and the identical shape to `OQ-WRK-1561`. **WHAT IS BUILT MEANWHILE, AND NOTHING WAITS:** the control stays visible and disabled with its reason (`BR-SHL-025` — a hidden control is not an authorisation), and `06-backend/tests/test_cr138_p4_17_site_visit_refusal.py` now RE-CHECKS the refusal's evidence on every run, so the day a rule is ratified or a seventh flow door is opened the suite goes RED and names the control to release. ⚠ **WHAT WOULD BE WRONG TO DO:** open the door and let a clerk book a visit on nobody's authority. A crew in a van costs a real utility real money, and `from_flows`'s first standing rule is that *the raise is OFFERED, never automatic*. | product (a fact only the utility and its regulator hold) | `CR-138-BRC07` `P4.17` · `EP-PAY-04` §S34 scope row · `apps/work/from_flows.FLOW_KINDS` · `apps/metering/connections.SOURCE_DUNNING_AUTO` · `apps/customers/disconnects.VISIT_NOT_SCHEDULED` · `OQ-WRK-1561` (the same shape, closed) · `CR-070` §2(a) | **nothing is blocked** — the control is disabled with its reason and the refusal is now re-checked by a test rather than believed | **CLOSED 2026-09-23** |
 | OQ-PAY-12599 | 🔒 **WHAT IS *"ITS BATCH"* WHEN A TILL HAS NO TENDER YET? TWO OF YOUR OWN RULINGS, ONE DAY APART, CANNOT BOTH BE OBEYED LITERALLY — AND THE SAFE READING IS BUILT AND RUNNING.** On **2026-09-21** you ruled *"When a counter is started a batch should be automatically started with it"* — singular, and the blueprint's outcome is *"One act, not two. The batch exists before the money does"* (p055). On **2026-09-22**, answering `OQ-PAY-12577`, you ruled that **a batch is one till × one tender** — Dana's shift taking $600 cash and $640 cheques is ONE till, TWO tenders and **two batches**. ⚠ **A till at the moment it opens has taken nothing, so the set of tenders it will take is not knowable**, and `deposit_batches.method_id` is NOT NULL: there is no such thing as a batch with no tender. **Three ways out were weighed and the measurement decided it.** **(a) open one batch for each hand tender — cash, cheque, money order.** Literal, and it strands rubbish for ever: `payments_deposit_batch_lock()` **REFUSES A DELETE BY NAME**, so a till that takes only cash leaves TWO empty `$0.00` in-transit batches that nothing can ever remove and that go on answering the bank-matching screen's candidate query. Three clerks × 250 shifts is **up to 1,500 undeletable empty rows a year** on the screen whose whole job is *ready to match*. **(b) open nothing; create each batch with the first payment of its tender.** Strands nothing and honours your stated PURPOSE — *"that removes a window in which a counter payment would have had no batch"* — because the batch is written inside that payment's own transaction. But it makes the drawn sentence false at the moment it is drawn about. **(c) — BUILT — open the session and ONE batch: the till's CASH batch.** Cash is what a drawer is made of: the opening float is denominated in it (*"Cash in the drawer before you start"*), the Close screen's first row is *"Cash incl. float"*, and it is the one tender present at the instant the till opens. The drawn sentence is then literally true and the stranding is bounded to **at most one** row, only for a till that took no cash at all. Every other tender's batch is opened by its first payment. **REVERSIBILITY DECIDED IT:** (a) wrong is permanent because the rows cannot be deleted; (c) wrong is one function. **The question in one line: is (c) what you meant by *a batch*, or should a till open one batch per hand tender and accept the empty ones?** Nothing is blocked — (c) is live. ⚠ **What was NOT used, and it is worth knowing:** `service_centres.payment_methods_accepted` looks like the tender set this needs and is not — it is portal vocabulary (`cheque`, not the tender code `check`), needs a translation nobody has signed, and one seeded centre publishes `[]`, which would have opened NO batch at all. | product | LANE P2-API, 2026-09-23, building `P2.1` of `CR-136-BRC07` | **Nothing — `(c)` is built and running. This is for CONFIRMATION, because it narrows one of your rulings with the other** | **OPEN** |
-| OQ-PAY-12600 | ⚠ **WHERE DOES A FORCED CLOSE'S REASON LIVE, NOW THAT `note` BELONGS TO THE LATE COUNT? TODAY IT LIVES ONLY IN `audit_log`, WHICH IS PRUNED — AND THE SCREEN CALLS IT *"the only record of the reason"*.** p057 draws `Why *` as required on Force close and says underneath: *"This is the only record of the reason."* Your ruling of 2026-09-23 §3.2 then gave `counter_sessions.note` to the DECLARE — *"Declaring fills counted_totals, difference and note — NULL on a forced close precisely because nobody counted"* — so writing the supervisor's reason there would be **silently overwritten by the late count's own note**, which is exactly the *"Nothing is erased"* the same ruling insists on. **So the reason is written to `audit_log` and nowhere else, and `audit_log` is pruned on a retention schedule** (`OQ-1340`, OPEN, the identical cost you accepted for a corrected cheque number on 2026-09-02). **Worked:** Dana's Monday till is force-closed by a supervisor with *"went home without closing"*; six months later the retention job runs; the row then says a supervisor sealed it and **nothing says why**. **Two ways out. (a)** a third nullable column on `counter_sessions` — `force_reason` — beside `P2.20`'s two, so the two facts never share a box; it is one migration and it is the smallest thing that makes p057's sentence true. **(b)** answer `OQ-1340` so a correction-and-reason audit row is EXEMPT from pruning, which fixes this and the cheque-number case together. **Recommended: (a)**, because it is local, it is cheap, and it does not wait on a retention policy. Meanwhile **no screen, document or docstring may describe a forced close as a permanent record of its reason.** | product | LANE P2-API, 2026-09-23, building `P2.2` of `CR-136-BRC07` | **`P2.6`'s Force close screen, which must not promise permanence. Nothing is blocked — the reason IS recorded, just not for ever** | **OPEN** |
-| OQ-PAY-12601 | 🔒 **A CHEQUE THAT ARRIVES IN THE POST HAS NO TILL, AND THE NEW RULE WOULD REFUSE IT. WHAT SHOULD A BACK-OFFICE CLERK DO?** Your ruling of 2026-09-23 §3.4, verbatim: *"(a) Refuse it. \"Start your counter first.\" One press, then take the money. It is the only option where every cash dollar belongs to somebody"*. **The case shown to you was cash at a counter**, and it is built exactly as ruled: `cash`, `check` and `money_order` are refused when the person recording them has no open till. ⚠ **A back-office clerk keying a cheque that arrived in the morning post never sits at a till**, so this rule refuses them too — a consequence that was not in front of you. **No silent exception has been built and the ruling has not been widened**; the refusal is deliberately wider than the case that was ruled on, and this row is the record of that. **Three ways out. (a)** a mailed cheque is banked under the day's SYSTEM till, exactly as ACH and lockbox already are — which is honest (*nobody counted this*) and needs no new concept, but means a cheque in the post and a cheque at the window are told apart by where the clerk was sitting rather than by anything recorded. **(b)** the back office opens a till too — one per day, per person, counted like any other; it is uniform and it asks a clerk who never touches a drawer to balance one. **(c)** the refusal is narrowed to `cash` alone, on the ground that a cheque is traceable and a banknote is not. ⚠ **Whichever way it goes, there is a SECOND half: two other hand-recording doors do not call the new guard today and are named rather than left to be found** — `POST /customers/{id}/payments` (a multi-account tender) and the transfer path. Both are inside the ruling's spirit and neither was in front of you, so neither was changed. **Recommended: (a) plus widening the guard to those two doors**, so the rule is one sentence with one implementation. | product | LANE P2-API, 2026-09-23, building `P2.23` of `CR-136-BRC07` | **Nothing today — the ruling is built as given. It bites the first time a utility keys a mailed cheque, and the refusal will be correct-as-ruled and wrong-in-fact** | **OPEN** |
-| OQ-PAY-12602 | **THE PAYMENT DETAIL'S *RECONCILED* CELL NAMES A REFERENCE THIS PRODUCT DOES NOT MINT — SHOULD A BANK-REC MATCH SET GET ITS OWN NUMBER SERIES?** ⚠ **NOTHING IS BLOCKED AND NOTHING IS STUBBED.** `GET /payments/{id}` serves the header strip today with every fact that IS established — the deposit batch's own `DEP-` code, when the tie was matched, how many bank lines and how many sibling batches it holds — and `reference: null` beside `match_set_has_no_reference_series` on `unavailable[]`. **WHAT THE DRAWING ASKS FOR:** screen 2·2 (p035) draws `BRM-2609-0041` under RECONCILED with the sub-line *"Three batches, one credit"*. **WHAT EXISTS:** `bank_rec_match_sets` has no code column and no series; `bankrec_read._match_set_panel` serves `match_set_id`, a bare uuid, and **B14 rule 31 forbids putting a database id on a screen** — so the drawn cell cannot be filled from anything the product holds. **THE QUESTION IN PLAIN ENGLISH:** when a bookkeeper ties three of your deposit slips to one line on the bank statement, does that tie deserve a number they can quote — the way a deposit batch gets `DEP-2609-0041` and a payment gets `PMT-2609-000418` — or is naming the batch enough? **WHY IT IS NOT OURS:** a new identifier series is a thing a utility reads out loud to its bank and its auditor, and `core.numbering` series are the PO's own catalogue (`CR-005`). Minting one from a lane would put a number in front of people that nobody agreed to. **WHAT IT COSTS TODAY:** the cell shows the batch code and the counts, and the tie itself is unnameable — a bookkeeper saying *"the one with three batches on 21 September"* rather than *"BRM-2609-0041"*. | product · identifier series | `CR-137-BRC07` `P3.1` · blueprint p035 ⑥ · `apps/payments/bankrec_read._match_set_panel` · `apps/payments/payment_detail._reconciled` · B14 rule 31 · `core.numbering` | **nothing is blocked** — the header strip serves the established facts and states the absence | 🔴 **OPEN — raised 2026-09-23** 