+"DEF-PAY-6238","2026-09-23","CR-137 P3-BACK lane","Cancel writes a negative money row and its service is on no guard list — the 🔒 question was never put for apps/payments/cancel.py","06-backend/apps/payments/cancel.py · 10-scripts/gates/human_owned.txt · 12-governance/09-human-review-ledger.md","payments","medium","CR-137-BRC07 P3-BACK, 2026-09-23, reading G53's output after landing P3.2","e9cc747c5 — the file is new; the guard question comes with it","check-that-does-not-check","G53 finds a file that CLAIMS the protocol in its docstring and has no glob. cancel.py claims nothing, so G53 cannot see it and reports truthfully over a scope that excludes it — hard-won law 7's fifth shape. Its mirror apps/payments/reversal.py IS on human_owned.txt for exactly this reason: both write a negative payment row against an original. Cancel writes no journal line (a processing payment posted none) and takes no idempotency key, which is arguably why it is lighter — but that is a judgment a person should make, not one a lane should make by staying silent.","open","","Put it to the PO: does apps/payments/cancel.py belong on 10-scripts/gates/human_owned.txt beside reversal.py? Adding a file is a PROCESS EVENT and the PO's call, not a lane's — G53 says so in its own message. If yes, the file gains HUMAN-OWNED + HUMAN-REVIEW-PENDING and a row in the review ledger stating in plain English what a person is being asked to rule on."
+"DEF-PAY-6239","2026-09-23","LANE P2-TILLWIRE","The CR-136 phase 2 PostgreSQL proof had never been run and could not have been: SEVEN independent faults, four of them incapable of passing","10-scripts/cr136_p2_counters_postgres_proof.py","payments","medium","2.335.0","ff1b3b3ee","check-that-does-not-check","Nothing in this repo runs a 10-scripts/ proof. It is not a pytest file, no gate imports it, and run_gates never sees it - so a 385-line script asserting four guarantees about RLS, a race, an aborted transaction and a ledger pair sat on the branch being CITED as evidence by the CR and by three defect rows, with no record of ever having executed. Every one of the seven faults would have surfaced in the first thirty seconds of a single run, and none of them could surface without one. Hard-won law 7's clause about proving a guard red in the harness that will run it has no counterpart for a guard that has no harness at all.","open","","The seven, in the order a run meets them: (1) _users() raised SystemExit needing two users - measured across five proof-db templates and clones, every one holds ONE tenant and ZERO users, so the precondition was unmeetable; (2) _actor() read user.role and identity.User has no role field - it is on TenantMembership - so parts B, C and D each raised AttributeError on their first line; (3) core_tenancy.set_current_tenant_id does not exist (it is set_current_tenant), in six places; (4) part A2 recreated the unique index over the two duplicate rows part A1 exists to produce, so A1 proving it red is what stopped A2 restoring it; (5) the race was SEQUENTIAL - the second INSERT blocks on the first's uncommitted row and left.commit() sat on the next line of the same thread, so it hung for ever (pg_stat_activity: active/Lock plus idle in transaction); (6) after threading it, Django refuses a cross-thread connection and that exception made A2 report PASS while the index was never consulted - a false pass, caught only because A1 then failed for the same reason; (7) every teardown said .delete() on a CounterSession, which PROTECT refuses once it has a batch, and the batch cannot be deleted either (trg_deposit_batch_lock), so part D - the only part about money - was unreachable. Fixed: the script provisions what it needs as _centre already did, the race runs on a thread with a BOUNDED join, A2 asserts the refusal NAMES uq_counter_sessions_open_owner rather than accepting any exception, teardown CLOSES rather than deletes, and part D compares uuid to str as strings. ALL PARTS NOW PASS on PostgreSQL 16.13. The durable fix is not in this file: a proof under 10-scripts/ that nothing ever executes is a claim, and the cheapest cure is that whoever cites one states the date they last ran it."
+"DEF-PAY-6243","2026-09-23","LANE P2-SCREENS (CR-136-BRC07 P2.6), self-reported","Three 'clean' type-checks were timeouts, so the branch carried three TypeScript errors for a whole session and every push reported green","07-frontend/src/features/payments/CountersTab.tsx + CounterDoors.tsx, and the lane's own tsc invocation","payments","high","2026-09-23, LANE P2-SCREENS, by running tsc with NO timeout wrapper and capturing its exit code","35f86b374 (the required timeZone/dateFormat on CountersTabProps) and f77dca804 (size=small, where the kit's sizes are default|sm|row)","check-that-does-not-check","The lane ran 'timeout 560 tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json | head -30'. Under load this box needs longer than 560s, so timeout killed tsc BEFORE it printed anything - and the pipeline's exit code is head's, which is 0. The result was an EMPTY output file and a zero exit, which is indistinguishable from a clean pass and was read as one THREE TIMES. It is hard-won law 7's 'structurally incapable of failing' shape, and the sibling of the npx-tsc trap the react-tailwind-css-ui-generator skill already warns about: that one fetches a newer compiler and aborts on a config error, this one is killed before it speaks. Nothing downstream could catch it: vitest and vite build both strip types with esbuild rather than checking them, so 35 passing cases and two successful production builds were all green over a red type-check.","open","","Never wrap tsc in 'timeout' and never pipe it to head. Redirect to a FILE and append the exit code in the same command - 'tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json > out.txt 2>&1; echo ""TSC EXIT=$?"" >> out.txt' - then refuse to call it green until the file literally contains 'TSC EXIT=0'. An empty output file with no exit line is a KILLED run, not a clean one. The general rule this is an instance of: a check whose output cannot distinguish pass from fail to a reader is worse than no check, because it is trusted."
+"DEF-SHL-6244","2026-09-23","LANE P2-SCREENS (CR-136-BRC07 P2.6), reading a red it did not cause","The ConfirmDialog tier census still lists AccountImportsTab, which stopped drawing one on 2026-09-22, so B14 rule 6's census test is red on a stale entry","07-frontend/src/components/ConfirmDialogTier.dom.test.tsx, the call-site census","platform-shell","low","2026-09-23, LANE P2-SCREENS, running the kit suites after touching FormModal","470e316f8 (CR-135-BRC07 P1.5, 2026-09-22) - the Imports tab moved to the shared run list and its ConfirmDialog went with it","stale-record","The census is a hand-maintained list of every ConfirmDialog call site, and it exists so B14 rule 6's danger tier cannot be forgotten at a new one. It has no half that notices a call site GOING AWAY - it only fails, and the failure names the file as though the file were wrong. P1.5 removed the last ConfirmDialog from AccountImportsTab.tsx and nothing pointed at the census; the test has been red since, and a red nobody owns is how a true red acquires the protective colouring of a known one.","open","","Dated with 'git log -S ConfirmDialog -- <file>', which is law 7's own one-command check for 'was this red before I started'. The census should be DERIVED from a grep of the call sites rather than typed, so a removed call site subtracts itself; until then, a lane removing a ConfirmDialog must edit the census in the same commit, exactly as a lane adding one must."
+"DEF-PAY-6245","2026-09-23","lane P2-HEAD","RecordCounterBanner's isError branch had no test: stubClient has offered {fail: true} since it was written and nothing ever passed it","07-frontend/src/features/payments/counterScreens.dom.test.tsx","payments","low","2.337.0","f77dca804","check-that-does-not-check","The fixture grew a fail option and no case used it, so the one branch that tells 'we could not ask' from 'you have no till' was never rendered by any test - on the form that takes money. It could not have been tested there either: that file renders STATICALLY and seeds each query's DATA into the cache, and an error arrives by rejecting a promise, so the query stays pending and the component returns null on its loading guard. Writing the case in that file produces one that can never pass, which reads as 'the branch is broken' rather than 'the harness cannot see it' - law 7's incapable-of-passing shape. Found while checking whether DEF-PAY-6235's fix could have broken the branch in silence; it does not, and the same case now proves that too.","open","","A fixture option nothing passes is a branch nobody has rendered - grep a stub's own options for their call sites. And an ERROR state cannot be seeded with setQueryData, so a suite that only renders statically cannot test one at all; it needs jsdom and a wait."
+"DEF-PAY-6246","2026-09-23","LANE P2-TILLWIRE","BE-05 drops an undeclared f.<name> filter SILENTLY, so a narrowed question is answered with the whole list and the caller cannot tell","06-backend/core/pagination.py::parse_list_params","tenancy","high","2.335.0","BE-05","wrong-logic","It is not a bug in any one endpoint, so no endpoint's tests cover it, and it is DOCUMENTED behaviour - parse_list_params carries the comment 'undeclared filter names are ignored' and BR-SHL-010's fall-back-or-clamp semantics are deliberate for sort and dir, where falling back to a default is harmless. A filter is not like a sort. Falling back on a sort gives you the same ROWS in a different order; falling back on a filter gives you DIFFERENT ROWS, more of them, and a caller that asked to be narrowed reads row 0 as though it had been. DEF-PAY-6235 (lane P2-HEAD's row) is what that costs on one screen: GET /counters?f.mine=1&per=1 answered the supervisor's list of everyone's open tills, and the payments page head announced a colleague's drawer as 'Your counter' beside a button that takes money. Nothing went red - the read succeeded, both states rendered, and per=1 made the wrong row arrive faster.","open","","Refused on GET /counters today by counters_api._refuse_an_unknown_filter, which is a local fix and says so: making parse_list_params refuse globally would change the answer of every list endpoint in the product on one screen's authority, and a client sending a stray f.* that has been quietly ignored for weeks would start 400-ing. THE QUESTION THIS ROW CARRIES: should an undeclared f.<name> refuse everywhere? Three shapes to weigh - (a) refuse, which is what this lane did locally and what makes a client bug visible at the moment it is written; (b) a strict_filters flag each endpoint opts into, which is safe and will be forgotten by exactly the endpoints that need it; (c) leave it and require every list's tests to assert the NARROWING rather than the shape, which is the check that would have caught this one. Whichever is chosen, the measurable thing first: count how many f.* names the front end sends today that no endpoint declares. If it is zero, (a) costs nothing."
+"DEF-PAY-6247","2026-09-23","LANE P2-TILLWIRE","DEF-PAY-6235 is TWO different defects: lane P2-HEAD's counter f.mine row and lane P4-READS' ladder audit row, both filed 2026-09-23","12-governance/10-defect-log.csv - DEF-PAY-6235 appears twice","payments","high","2.335.0","2026-09-23","lane-collision","allocate.py and file_defect.py both ask the REMOTE for the maximum, and CLAUDE.md law 11 already states what that cannot do: nothing writes to the remote, so two lanes computing max+1 in the same minute get the same answer and both are right. The register is append-only, so the two appends never conflict and git merged both cleanly. Nothing downstream asks whether an id appears twice - a duplicate in a 3,744-row register reads as a register, not as an error. It was found only because close_defect.py REFUSES to edit an ambiguous row; a tool declining to act on bad data is what surfaced it, not any check that looks for it. This lane hit the same collision twice more in the same hour - DEF-PAY-6243 and DEF-PAY-6245 were both taken by sibling lanes between allocation and push, and were renumbered before pushing because push_paths.sh refused the stale register.","open","","NOT renumbered by this lane, deliberately: both DEF-PAY-6235 rows are cited from other lanes' code and comments, and picking one to move is a mass citation rewrite taken on a third lane's authority. The two owners should agree which keeps 6235. What would catch it: G17 already fails a duplicate CR NUMBER even when the branch suffixes differ - the same check over the defect register, run at push time rather than at read time, is the layer that ensures it. Cheapest interim, and it is one read: file_defect.py re-reads the register it has just appended to and refuses if its own new id now appears twice, at the only moment the answer can be final."
